<section class="w-full max-w-4xl mx-auto px-4 sm:px-6 py-8">
  <div class="space-y-6">
    <!-- Main Educational Card -->
    <div class="rounded-3xl atelier-card p-6 sm:p-8 space-y-6 border border-slate-200 shadow-sm">
      <div class="space-y-2">
        <span class="text-xs font-semibold font-mono text-amber-700 uppercase tracking-wider">
          Gemological Standards & History
        </span>
        <h2 class="text-xl sm:text-2xl font-bold tracking-tight text-slate-900 font-display" data-i18n="explainerTitle">
          Understanding Ratti vs Carat in Indian Gemology
        </h2>
      </div>

      <div class="grid grid-cols-1 md:grid-cols-2 gap-6 text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-600 leading-relaxed">
        <div class="space-y-3">
          <h3 class="font-bold text-slate-900 text-sm flex items-center gap-2">
            <span>🌱</span> The Ancient Gunja Seed Origin
          </h3>
          <p>
            The term <strong>Ratti</strong> originates from the scarlet seed of the <em>Abrus precatorius</em> (Gunja) plant in ancient India. Due to its remarkable consistency in weight (~120–180 mg), Vedic traders utilized these seeds as the foundational unit for weighing diamonds, rubies, and gold.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div class="space-y-3">
          <h3 class="font-bold text-slate-900 text-sm flex items-center gap-2">
            <span>💎</span> The Metric Carat Standard (1907)
          </h3>
          <p>
            Adopted internationally at the 4th General Conference on Weights and Measures in 1907, the <strong>Metric Carat (Ct)</strong> is defined precisely as <strong>200 milligrams (0.200 grams)</strong>. Today, international labs (GIA, IGI, GRS) issue certificates exclusively in Metric Carats.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>

      <!-- Pakka vs Sunee Explainer Box -->
      <div class="p-5 rounded-2xl bg-amber-50/70 border border-amber-200/80 space-y-2">
        <h4 class="text-xs font-bold text-amber-900 uppercase tracking-wider flex items-center gap-2">
          <span>⚖️</span> Pakka Ratti vs Sunee Ratti: The Critical Difference
        </h4>
        <p class="text-xs text-slate-700 leading-relaxed">
          In Northern and Western India, trade markets differentiate between <strong>Pakka Ratti (0.91 Carats / 182 mg)</strong> used by certified gem testing laboratories, and <strong>Sunee / Trade Ratti (0.90 Carats / 180 mg)</strong> historically used in local bazaars. To prevent under-dosing astrological prescriptions, always verify that your jeweler calibrates using the certified <strong>0.9100 standard</strong>.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>
